course: dictionary
lesson: 8516
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Satisfy has several meanings, so the context tells you which one you need. Read the words around it first. «The smell of the dust they kicked up was rich and satisfying.» Satisfying here means pleasing, something that makes you feel content.
explain: Satisfy can also mean to make hunger or thirst go away: a good meal satisfies hunger. In a maths sum, a number satisfies an equation when it makes the equation true. In x + 3 = 7, the number 4 satisfies it, because 4 + 3 = 7.
explain: Satisfy can also mean to meet a rule, need or condition. In a story, «curiosity was satisfied» means the character finally found out what they wanted to know. Always ask: what is being satisfied? A person, a hunger, a rule or a sum? Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check it still makes sense.
fact: Satisfy is a verb: satisfy, satisfies, satisfied, satisfying.
fact: To satisfy hunger or thirst is to make it go away.
fact: To satisfy a rule, need or condition is to meet it.
fact: A number satisfies an equation if it makes the equation true.
fact: To satisfy yourself that something is true is to make sure of it.
exercise: A good meal can satisfy your hunger. What does satisfy mean here? | Make your hunger grow bigger | Cook the meal again | Tell someone about your hunger | *Make your hunger go away
exercise: In x + 3 = 7, which number satisfies the equation? | 10 | 5 | *4 | 3
exercise: To satisfy the rules, what must you do? | Forget them | Write them down twice | Break them on purpose | *Meet them
test: «The smell of the dust they kicked up was rich and satisfying.» What does satisfying mean here? | Boring and dull | *Pleasing and making you content | Dusty and dirty | Noisy and loud
test: Mia wanted to know why the lamp kept flickering, and at last she found out. What does this show? | *Her curiosity was satisfied | She lost the lamp | Her curiosity was ignored | She stopped being curious about rules
exam: Satisfy yourself means what? | *To make sure something is true | To make someone angry | To dislike a meal | To forget a rule
